"""The seam between Workpeg AI and whatever actually runs a sandbox.

A sandbox is a container the agent drives on somebody's behalf. The mechanics
already exist in this SDK — ``workpeg.run.run_with_docker`` builds a project
and starts it — but that function is *install*-shaped: it takes a directory
with a Dockerfile, publishes a port, joins a named network and sets
``--restart unless-stopped``. None of that is what a sandbox wants, and two
parts of it are exactly what a sandbox must never have. So this is a new
seam rather than a wider signature on the old one.

**Hardening is not a parameter.** The one thing this module exists to make
impossible is creating a sandbox that is missing a control. Every safety
property — no network, dropped capabilities, read-only root, a non-root user,
no new privileges, pids/memory/CPU/swap limits, no restart policy — is
applied by the driver on every create, with no argument that can turn any of
them off and no argument that can be forgotten. What a caller *may* choose is
narrow and bounded: which image, whose sandbox it is, how big the limits are
within a validated range, and how long it lives.

**Networking is a closed choice, not a string.** ``workpeg_net`` puts a
container on the same bridge as Postgres, Redis and the API with no
credentials needed, and a driver that took a network *name* would be one
typo away from that. So the choice is an enum with two members and neither
of them can express it: :attr:`Network.NONE` is no interface at all, and
:attr:`Network.ISOLATED` is a per-owner bridge created ``--internal``, which
Docker itself refuses to route off the host. There is no third option and no
escape hatch.

**No environment reaches the container.** Secrets are meant to be injected at
an egress proxy so that dumping a sandbox's environment yields nothing, and
the cheapest way to keep that true is to have no way to pass an environment
at all. ``create`` takes no env, no volumes and no mounts, and a test asserts
the built argv contains none of those flags. A sandbox image that needs a
variable declares it in its own Dockerfile, where it is a public fact about
the image rather than a per-run secret.

**A sandbox is named by its owner.** One per staff member, reused across
their chats, so the container name is derived from the staff id and a second
create raises :class:`SandboxExists` naming the one they already have. The
owner is validated to a strict slug before it becomes a name, because that
name is what every later command addresses — an owner containing a dot or a
leading hyphen could otherwise choose which container, or which *flag*, the
platform sends. The name is built from validated identifiers, never from a
request string, the same way the hook path's ``_resolve_hook_urn`` is.

**The transport is ``docker exec``, not an HTTP ``/invoke``** (reversed
2026-08-13; PRD §12 #4). The first two attempts at this decision were made
on incomplete cost analysis. ``/invoke`` was chosen for having no socket in
the request path, at a stated cost of "no live output streaming"; building
4.1 found it also required the sandbox to be on *some* network, contradicting
§6.1 and §10; building 4.3 found that even that does not work, because a
container the DinD sidecar creates lives in *the sidecar's* network
namespace and is not routable from the API at all. ``docker exec`` goes
through the **daemon API**, which the platform already reaches over TLS — so
the network-namespace problem does not need solving, it disappears.

Three things follow, and they are the point of the reversal:

* A sandbox can go back to **no network at all**, which is what §6.1 and §10
  asked for in the first place. :attr:`Network.NONE` is the default again
  and is now a usable configuration rather than one that silently could not
  run anything.
* There is an attached process, so **output can stream** (4.7).
* This interface can name a program, so ``sandbox.*`` tools become worth
  registering (4.6).

**The cost is that ``argv`` exists, and it is a list.** A model chooses what
goes in it. There is therefore no shell anywhere on this path: no ``sh -c``,
no interpolation, no string that is split on whitespace. A shell string
would make every metacharacter in a model's output executable, so a string
is refused outright by :func:`validate_argv` rather than escaped — escaping
is a thing that can be got wrong, and refusing is not. Metacharacters
*inside* a list element are inert (there is nothing to interpret them) and
are deliberately passed through, because refusing them would break
``python -c`` while buying nothing.
"""
import codecs
import logging
import re
import time
from dataclasses import dataclass, replace
from enum import Enum
from typing import Callable, Protocol, runtime_checkable

#: The platform's own bridge — Postgres, Redis and the API. Named here only
#: so it can be asserted absent: nothing a sandbox runs on may ever be this,
#: and :class:`Network` is deliberately unable to express it.
PLATFORM_NETWORK = "workpeg_net"

#: The uid:gid a sandbox runs as. ``nobody``, and not configurable — a
#: caller who could pick this could pick ``0``.
SANDBOX_USER = "65534:65534"

#: The only writable path in the container. The root filesystem is read-only,
#: so something has to be writable or most images fail on their first log
#: line; this is that, mounted small and with execution turned off so a
#: dropped payload cannot be run from it.
TMPFS_SPEC = "/tmp:rw,noexec,nosuid,nodev,size=64m"

#: Label prefix on every container this driver creates. The reaper enumerates
#: by it. Deliberately carries only stable facts (that it is a sandbox, and
#: whose) — never the deadline, which changes and which Docker cannot update
#: on an existing container.
LABEL = "com.workpeg.sandbox"

#: Limit ranges. The floors stop a caller from configuring a sandbox that
#: cannot start; the ceilings stop one person's sandbox from being the
#: whole host. Neither end can be waived, and there is no value that means
#: "unlimited" — that absence is the point.
MIN_MEMORY_MB, MAX_MEMORY_MB = 64, 4096
MIN_CPUS, MAX_CPUS = 0.1, 4.0
MIN_PIDS, MAX_PIDS = 16, 1024

#: How long a sandbox may be held before the reaper is entitled to destroy
#: it. The ceiling is low on purpose: the cost of an idle per-staff sandbox
#: is an open question, and until it is answered the driver refuses to hold
#: one for a working day.
DEFAULT_TTL = 900.0
MIN_TTL, MAX_TTL = 30.0, 3600.0

#: Bounds one exec. A sandbox is not trusted to finish, or to stop.
DEFAULT_EXEC_TIMEOUT = 20.0
MIN_EXEC_TIMEOUT, MAX_EXEC_TIMEOUT = 1.0, 120.0

#: How much output is read before the rest is dropped and the process is
#: killed. What a command prints is the single most attacker-controlled
#: string in the feature — it reaches a model's context and a person's
#: screen — so it is bounded before it is anything else. The bound is on the
#: READ, not on a buffer somebody trims afterwards: a command that prints a
#: gigabyte must cost this process a bounded amount of memory, not a
#: gigabyte it then throws most of away.
MAX_OUTPUT_BYTES = 64 * 1024

#: How many words a command may have, and how long one may be. Neither
#: number is interesting in itself; what matters is that both exist. An argv
#: is assembled by a model and handed to ``execve``, which has a hard total
#: limit of its own — hitting it would be an obscure ``E2BIG`` at request
#: time instead of a sentence saying the command was too long.
MAX_ARGV_LEN = 256
MAX_ARG_BYTES = 8 * 1024

#: What may be written to a command's standard input.
#:
#: The ceiling is deliberately well under a pipe's 64 KiB kernel buffer, and
#: that is load-bearing rather than cautious: the runner writes stdin in one
#: call, closes it, and only then starts reading output. That order cannot
#: deadlock **because** the write is guaranteed to fit in the buffer and
#: return without waiting for a reader. Raise this above 64 KiB and a
#: command that prints before it finishes reading would hang both ends.
MAX_STDIN_BYTES = 32 * 1024

#: A staff id, as it will appear in a container name. Lowercase
#: alphanumerics and hyphens: enough for a uuid or a numeric id, and short of
#: anything that could make the name read as another container or as a flag.
_OWNER_RE = re.compile(r"[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]{0,62}\Z")

#: An image reference. Refuses a leading hyphen, which is the shape that
#: turns an image argument into an extra flag.
_IMAGE_RE = re.compile(r"[a-z0-9][a-zA-Z0-9._/:@-]{0,254}\Z")

#: Control characters, except the tab and newline real output contains.
#: Everything a sandbox writes is untrusted third-party text.
_CONTROL_RE = re.compile(r"[\x00-\x08\x0b-\x1f\x7f-\x9f]")

#: How much of a command's deadline the output consumer may spend before it
#: is cut off, and stops being called for the rest of that command.
#:
#: A consumer holds the read loop while it runs, on the thread that called
#: ``exec``, so every second it spends is a second the command does not get
#: and a second the caller does not get back. Without a share of its own it
#: spends the whole deadline and more:
#: :func:`validate_exec_timeout` exists because "an unbounded exec is a
#: worker thread the platform never gets back", and a consumer running
#: inside the read loop is exactly how that comes back. Cutting it off costs
#: the tail of somebody's transcript and costs the command nothing, which is
#: the right way round — see :class:`OutputRelay`.
#:
#: It is one number rather than two because the cut-off and the blame are
#: the same event: a timeout is allowed to name the consumer exactly when
#: the consumer had already been stopped for taking this much. Half, because
#: below that the command was going to be late anyway, and a message that
#: blamed the consumer for every timeout is a message nobody reads.
SLOW_CONSUMER_SHARE = 0.5

#: This package's logger, and the SDK's first. It exists for one thing:
#: :class:`OutputRelay` hands untrusted output to a callback somebody else
#: wrote, and a callback that raises must neither kill the command nor
#: disappear. ``print`` is a CLI habit rather than a library's, and
#: ``warnings`` is deduplicated by default and routed nowhere a worker's
#: logs are read, so the failure goes where a library's failures go.
logger = logging.getLogger(__name__)


class SandboxError(Exception):
    """Anything this driver refused or could not do."""


class SandboxConfigError(SandboxError, ValueError):
    """A sandbox the driver will not create as asked."""


class SandboxExists(SandboxError):
    """This owner already has a sandbox.

    Carries :attr:`name` so the caller can say *which one* rather than only
    that there was one — a sandbox is per staff member and reused across
    their chats, so the answer to a second create is the first one.
    """

    def __init__(self, name):
        super().__init__(
            f"{name} already exists. A sandbox is per staff member and is "
            f"reused across their chats; use the one they have or destroy "
            f"it first.")
        self.name = name


class SandboxNotRunning(SandboxError):
    """There was no running container to run the command in.

    This replaces the ``SandboxUnreachable`` the HTTP transport raised, and
    the rename is the point rather than tidying. "Unreachable" described a
    request that went out and got nothing back; nothing here goes over a
    network, so the only way a command cannot run is that the container is
    stopped or gone. That is not a bad gateway and it is not a retry — it is
    a sandbox to destroy and recreate, and an error named for what happened
    is what makes a caller answer that way.

    It is raised on the daemon's authority, never by reading an error
    message: when a command exits non-zero the driver asks the daemon what
    state the container is in. A sandbox printing "Error response from
    daemon" to its own output therefore cannot talk the platform into
    believing it has died.
    """

    def __init__(self, name):
        super().__init__(
            f"{name} is not running, so there was nothing to run the command "
            f"in. Destroy it and create another.")
        self.name = name


class SandboxTimeout(SandboxError):
    """A docker command did not return in time."""


class Network(Enum):
    """What a sandbox is attached to.

    Two members, and neither is a name a caller supplies. ``NONE`` is the
    default and is a container with no interfaces: nothing in, nothing out.
    ``ISOLATED`` is a per-owner bridge created ``--internal``, which Docker
    will not route beyond itself.

    **``NONE`` is a working configuration again.** Under the HTTP transport
    it was not: a container with no interfaces had nothing to POST to, so
    the one thing §6.1 and §10 both mandate was the one thing that could not
    run a command. ``docker exec`` reaches the container through the daemon
    rather than through the network, so a sandbox needs no interface at all
    and the default is now both the safest option and a usable one.

    ``ISOLATED`` survives for the job it is genuinely needed for: §10's
    egress proxy, where credentials are injected outside the container so a
    dumped environment yields nothing. Nothing in this module requires it.

    There is no member for the platform bridge and there is no member that
    takes a string, so :data:`PLATFORM_NETWORK` is not expressible here.
    """

    NONE = "none"
    ISOLATED = "isolated"


class State(Enum):
    """Where a sandbox is, as far as the daemon is concerned."""

    #: The container exists and is running.
    RUNNING = "running"
    #: The container exists and is not running. It still occupies a name and
    #: still has to be destroyed.
    STOPPED = "stopped"
    #: No such container. The only state that tells a caller to stop trying
    #: — a reaper stops sweeping for it and the API deletes the row that
    #: remembers it — so it is said only on a backend's own authority. A
    #: backend that could not be reached is not this; it is an exception.
    GONE = "gone"


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Limits:
    """What one sandbox may consume.

    Validated on construction rather than at use, so an out-of-range limit
    fails where it was written. Every field has a floor and a ceiling and
    none of them accepts a value meaning "no limit".
    """

    memory_mb: int = 512
    cpus: float = 1.0
    pids: int = 128

    def __post_init__(self):
        _bounded_int(self.memory_mb, "memory_mb",
                     MIN_MEMORY_MB, MAX_MEMORY_MB)
        _bounded_float(self.cpus, "cpus", MIN_CPUS, MAX_CPUS)
        _bounded_int(self.pids, "pids", MIN_PIDS, MAX_PIDS)


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Handle:
    """A sandbox that exists, as everything else refers to it.

    Plain data: the driver keeps nothing, so a handle is what a caller
    persists (as the ``Sandbox`` row) and hands back.

    **There is no ``endpoint``.** The HTTP transport put one here — a URL
    built from the container name — and it is gone rather than left as
    ``None``, because a field that is always empty is one a later reader
    tries to fill in. Nothing addresses a sandbox by URL any more; the
    ``name`` is what the daemon is asked about, and the daemon is the only
    thing that talks to the container.
    """

    owner: str
    name: str
    container_id: str
    image: str
    network: Network
    limits: Limits
    created_at: float
    expires_at: float

    def is_expired(self, now: "float | None" = None) -> bool:
        return (time.time() if now is None else now) >= self.expires_at


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Status:
    """What the daemon says about a sandbox right now."""

    state: State
    container_id: str
    exit_code: "int | None" = None


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class ExecResult:
    """What came back from running a command.

    ``output`` is capped and scrubbed before it gets here, and ``truncated``
    says whether that happened — a caller that shows the text needs to know
    it is not the whole of it. Standard error is merged into it, in order,
    because what somebody wants to read is what the command printed rather
    than which file descriptor it chose.

    **``exit_code``, not ``status``.** The HTTP transport returned an HTTP
    status here, where success was ``200``; this is a process exit code,
    where success is ``0``. Keeping the old name would have left every
    ``if result.status == 200`` reading as correct while meaning the exact
    opposite, so the field is renamed and the rename is the migration.
    """

    exit_code: int
    output: str
    truncated: bool = False
    duration: float = 0.0

    @property
    def ok(self) -> bool:
        """The command succeeded — which is ``0``, not ``200``."""
        return self.exit_code == 0


@runtime_checkable
class SandboxDriver(Protocol):
    """What Workpeg AI needs from a sandbox backend.

    Docker today, a microVM later, chosen by configuration. The protocol is
    the part that must not change when that swap happens, which is why it
    carries no Docker vocabulary: no flags, no socket, no command line.
    ``argv`` is the one word here that sounds like an implementation detail
    and is not — it is the POSIX shape every backend will execute, and a
    microVM runs a list of strings for exactly the same reasons.

    There is no argument anywhere that weakens isolation: an implementation
    applies the full hardening set on every create, and runs every command
    as the sandbox's unprivileged user, or it is not an implementation of
    this.
    """

    def create(self, *, owner: str, image: str,
               limits: "Limits | None" = None,
               network: Network = Network.NONE,
               ttl: float = DEFAULT_TTL) -> Handle:
        """Start a sandbox for ``owner``, or raise if they have one."""

    def exec(self, handle: Handle, argv: list, *,
             timeout: float = DEFAULT_EXEC_TIMEOUT,
             stdin: "bytes | str | None" = None,
             on_output: "Callable[[str], None] | None" = None) -> ExecResult:
        """Run ``argv`` inside the sandbox, as its unprivileged user.

        ``argv`` is a list of strings and there is no overload that takes a
        string. That is the whole security posture of this method: a model
        assembles the list, so anything that turned one string into several
        — a shell, a split on whitespace — would make the model's output
        executable. There is no such step, here or in any implementation of
        this.

        ``on_output``, when it is given, is handed each piece of what the
        command printed **as the command prints it**, rather than once at
        the end. It is the only reason anything here can be shown live; the
        result is still the whole answer and is still returned. Every clause
        of the contract below is load-bearing, and an implementation that
        keeps only some of them is not an implementation of this.

        **It is called synchronously, on the calling thread, from inside
        this method, and never after it returns.** Not from a reader thread,
        not through a queue, not once at the end. A caller's ordering can
        rest on that and the platform's does: its frame writer appends to a
        buffer that a generator is suspended in the middle of, so a chunk
        delivered from any other thread would land *after* the frames saying
        the call had finished — and nothing in this package could see that
        happen. The one thread an implementation may have is a watchdog that
        does nothing but end the command.

        **It takes one positional string and its return value is ignored.**
        One positional argument, so a function already bound to something
        else — ``partial(stream_output, message_id)`` — is a consumer with
        no adapter in between. The return value is ignored so that there is
        exactly one control surface: a consumer that no longer wants the
        text stops using it, and never stops the command, which would change
        the exit code for the sake of a display.

        **Standard error is merged into it, in order**, for the reason
        :class:`ExecResult` gives — what somebody reads is what the command
        printed, not which descriptor it chose. There is no argument that
        separates them, and the merge is what makes the ORDER true.

        **Everything it was handed joins up to ``result.output``**, exactly,
        when this returns normally: same cap, same lenient decode, same
        control-character strip. A person watching and a model reading must
        not be shown different text, so nothing past the cap is ever handed
        over and a character split across two reads is handed over whole.

        **A consumer that raises does not fail the command.** The command
        keeps running, its whole output still comes back on the result, and
        the consumer is not called again for the rest of that command — one
        that raised on one piece raises on all of them, and a traceback per
        piece is its own outage. The failure is logged rather than silently
        dropped.

        **Its time comes out of the command's, and it gets a share of the
        deadline rather than all of it.** A consumer holds the read loop
        while it runs, so a slow one can turn a command that would have
        finished into a timeout. Two things bound that, and an
        implementation owes both. Once the deadline has passed nothing
        further is handed over: the command has been killed and its result
        is about to be thrown away, so a read after that reaches nobody
        through the result either, and handing it on would spend a caller's
        thread on a display for an answer nobody will see. And once a
        consumer has spent :data:`SLOW_CONSUMER_SHARE` of the deadline it is
        not called again for the rest of that command.

        **Neither of those bounds a call already in progress, and nothing
        here can.** The consumer runs on the calling thread — which is the
        clause above, and the one the platform's ordering rests on — so
        there is no thread left to interrupt it from. ``exec`` therefore
        returns at the deadline plus however long the consumer's current
        call takes, and a consumer that never returns holds the thread for
        as long as it does not. That is why "hand the text on" is the whole
        job: a consumer that does I/O must bound that I/O itself, because
        this cannot do it for them. It must not call back into the driver.

        **Streaming is a side effect that survives a failed call.** What was
        handed over before a timeout or a stopped container has been handed
        over; nothing retracts it.
        """

    def status(self, handle: Handle) -> Status:
        """What the backend says about this sandbox now.

        **Every :class:`Status` this returns is something the backend
        said.** "I could not ask" is not a state and must never be reported
        as one — least of all as :attr:`State.GONE`, which is the answer a
        caller acts on by deleting the record that is its only handle on the
        sandbox. An implementation that cannot reach its backend raises
        :class:`SandboxError` or :class:`SandboxTimeout`, which says "I do
        not know" and can be retried.

        This is written into the protocol rather than left to each driver
        because the Docker one got it wrong: a failed ``docker inspect``
        was read as ``GONE``, which is also exactly what an unreachable
        daemon looks like.
        """

    def keepalive(self, handle: Handle, ttl: float) -> Handle:
        """Push the deadline out, returning the handle to persist.

        This returns a handle rather than nothing, and the difference
        matters. A driver holds no state, and the one place it could write a
        deadline — a container label — cannot be changed after create. A
        ``keepalive`` that returned nothing would therefore have nowhere to
        put the new deadline: it would do exactly nothing, while looking
        from the call site like it had worked, and the reaper would destroy
        a sandbox somebody was using. The caller owns the deadline; this
        computes it.
        """

    def destroy(self, handle: Handle) -> None:
        """Remove the sandbox and anything created alongside it.

        Idempotent: destroying one that is already gone is not an error,
        because the reaper and a person clicking a button race by design.
        """


def _bounded_int(value, name, low, high):
    if isinstance(value, bool) or not isinstance(value, int):
        raise SandboxConfigError(f"{name} must be an integer")
    if not low <= value <= high:
        raise SandboxConfigError(
            f"{name} is {value}; it must be between {low} and {high}. There "
            f"is no value meaning unlimited — a sandbox without this limit "
            f"is one person's chat taking the host down.")
    return value


def _bounded_float(value, name, low, high):
    if isinstance(value, bool) or not isinstance(value, (int, float)):
        raise SandboxConfigError(f"{name} must be a number")
    if not low <= value <= high:
        raise SandboxConfigError(
            f"{name} is {value}; it must be between {low} and {high}. There "
            f"is no value meaning unlimited — a sandbox without this limit "
            f"is one person's chat taking the host down.")
    return float(value)


def validate_owner(owner) -> str:
    """The staff id a sandbox belongs to.

    Strict because this string becomes a container name, and a container
    name is an argument on a daemon command line. A leading hyphen would
    make it a flag; a slash or a dot would let the owner name a container
    that is not theirs. It is the hook path's SSRF problem arriving through
    a different door, and it is answered the same way — by refusing the
    shape rather than escaping it.
    """
    if not isinstance(owner, str) or not _OWNER_RE.fullmatch(owner):
        raise SandboxConfigError(
            f"owner {owner!r} must be lowercase letters, digits and hyphens, "
            f"starting with an alphanumeric. It becomes a container name and "
            f"therefore an argument to the daemon, so it cannot contain a "
            f"dot, a slash, or anything that could name something else.")
    return owner


def validate_image(image) -> str:
    """The image a sandbox runs.

    Refuses the shapes that stop being an image and start being an argument
    — anything with a leading hyphen, whitespace, or a character a reference
    cannot contain.
    """
    if not isinstance(image, str) or not _IMAGE_RE.fullmatch(image):
        raise SandboxConfigError(
            f"image {image!r} is not a usable image reference. It is passed "
            f"to the daemon as an argument, so a value that could be read as "
            f"a flag is refused rather than escaped.")
    return image


def validate_network(network) -> Network:
    """The attachment, which must be a :class:`Network` member.

    A string is refused even when it happens to match a member's value. The
    whole reason this is an enum is that a network *name* is one typo from
    the platform bridge, and accepting names here would give that typo back.
    """
    if not isinstance(network, Network):
        raise SandboxConfigError(
            f"network must be a Network member, not {network!r}. It is a "
            f"closed choice on purpose: a sandbox on {PLATFORM_NETWORK} "
            f"shares a bridge with Postgres, Redis and the API and needs no "
            f"credentials to reach any of them.")
    return network


def validate_ttl(ttl) -> float:
    return _bounded_float(ttl, "ttl", MIN_TTL, MAX_TTL)


def validate_exec_timeout(timeout) -> float:
    """The deadline on one command. Required, bounded, never ``None``.

    ``None`` is what every waiting API in the standard library reads as
    "wait forever", and a sandbox is not trusted to finish — an unbounded
    exec is a worker thread the platform never gets back.
    """
    return _bounded_float(
        timeout, "timeout", MIN_EXEC_TIMEOUT, MAX_EXEC_TIMEOUT)


def validate_argv(argv) -> list:
    """The command to run, which is a list of strings and nothing else.

    Read the refusals in order, because they are ordered by how badly the
    thing they refuse would go.

    **A string is refused first and by name.** It is the only input here
    that would *appear* to work: ``"rm -rf /"`` is iterable, so a validator
    that merely checked for a sequence would accept it and hand ``execve``
    the program ``r`` with the arguments ``m``, ``space``, ``-``… Refusing
    it with a sentence about shells is worth more than a type error,
    because the caller who passed it is the caller who was thinking in
    shell.

    **A NUL is refused.** Every other byte a model can produce is inert in
    an argument — there is no shell, so ``;`` and ``$(…)`` and a newline are
    just characters. ``\\x00`` is not inert: it terminates a C string, so
    Python and ``execve`` would disagree about where the argument ends.
    That disagreement is the whole of the smuggling risk on this path, and
    it is one character wide.

    **A leading hyphen in the program name is refused.** Docker's exec
    parser stops reading its own flags at the container name, and this
    driver also passes ``--`` before it, so an ``argv[0]`` of
    ``--privileged`` is inert today — measured, not assumed. It is still
    refused, because "inert today" is a property of a parser this project
    does not own, and no program is named that anyway.

    Everything else is passed through untouched and the tests say so
    explicitly, so that nobody later mistakes the absence of an escaping
    routine for an oversight.
    """
    if isinstance(argv, (str, bytes, bytearray)):
        raise SandboxConfigError(
            f"argv is a string ({argv!r:.60}). It has to be a list of "
            f"strings — ['ls', '-la'], not 'ls -la'. Nothing on this path "
            f"splits a string into words or runs a shell, so a string would "
            f"be one program with a very unusual name.")
    if not isinstance(argv, (list, tuple)):
        raise SandboxConfigError(
            f"argv must be a list of strings, not {type(argv).__name__}. It "
            f"is consumed once and inspected before it is run, so anything "
            f"that can only be iterated is refused rather than exhausted.")
    if not argv:
        raise SandboxConfigError(
            "argv is empty, so it names no program to run.")
    if len(argv) > MAX_ARGV_LEN:
        raise SandboxConfigError(
            f"argv has {len(argv)} words; the limit is {MAX_ARGV_LEN}.")

    for index, word in enumerate(argv):
        if not isinstance(word, str):
            raise SandboxConfigError(
                f"argv[{index}] is a {type(word).__name__}; every word must "
                f"be a string. Nothing here converts one for you: a value "
                f"that had to be coerced is a value somebody has not decided "
                f"the meaning of.")
        if "\x00" in word:
            raise SandboxConfigError(
                f"argv[{index}] contains a NUL byte. It would end the "
                f"argument at a different place for execve than it does for "
                f"Python, which is the one way a word here can smuggle "
                f"something past this check.")
        try:
            encoded = word.encode("utf-8")
        except UnicodeEncodeError as exc:
            raise SandboxConfigError(
                f"argv[{index}] is not encodable as UTF-8, so there is no "
                f"byte string to hand the operating system.") from exc
        if len(encoded) > MAX_ARG_BYTES:
            raise SandboxConfigError(
                f"argv[{index}] is {len(encoded)} bytes; the limit is "
                f"{MAX_ARG_BYTES}.")

    if not argv[0]:
        raise SandboxConfigError("argv[0] is empty, so it names no program.")
    if argv[0].startswith("-"):
        raise SandboxConfigError(
            f"argv[0] is {argv[0]!r}, which is the shape of a flag rather "
            f"than the name of a program. It is refused here even though the "
            f"daemon's parser has stopped reading its own flags by that "
            f"point, because that is a parser this project does not own.")
    return list(argv)


def validate_stdin(stdin) -> "bytes | None":
    """What is written to the command's standard input, if anything.

    Bounded well below a pipe's buffer so the write cannot block — see
    :data:`MAX_STDIN_BYTES`, where that bound is what makes the runner's
    write-then-read order deadlock-free.
    """
    if stdin is None:
        return None
    if isinstance(stdin, str):
        try:
            raw = stdin.encode("utf-8")
        except UnicodeEncodeError as exc:
            raise SandboxConfigError(
                "stdin is not encodable as UTF-8.") from exc
    elif isinstance(stdin, (bytes, bytearray)):
        raw = bytes(stdin)
    else:
        raise SandboxConfigError(
            f"stdin must be bytes, str or None, not {type(stdin).__name__}.")
    if len(raw) > MAX_STDIN_BYTES:
        raise SandboxConfigError(
            f"stdin is {len(raw)} bytes; the limit is {MAX_STDIN_BYTES}.")
    return raw


def validate_on_output(on_output) -> "Callable[[str], None] | None":
    """Where a running command's output goes, if anybody is listening.

    Checked in ``exec``, before the daemon is touched, for the same reason
    every other validator is: a refused command must not be one that
    started, printed, and was then stopped. Left until the first read it
    would be a ``TypeError`` per read, caught by :class:`OutputRelay`'s own
    guard and latched off — a command that streamed nothing at all, and said
    why only in a log.
    """
    if on_output is None or callable(on_output):
        return on_output
    raise SandboxConfigError(
        f"on_output must be callable or None, not {type(on_output).__name__}."
        f" It is handed each piece of a running command's output as text, "
        f"one positional argument, and its return value is ignored.")


def container_name(owner: str) -> str:
    """The one container name this owner's sandbox may have."""
    return f"wp-sbx-{validate_owner(owner)}"


def network_name(owner: str) -> str:
    """The isolated bridge this owner's sandbox lives on, if any."""
    return f"wp-sbx-net-{validate_owner(owner)}"


def scrub_output(raw: bytes) -> str:
    """Sandbox output, made safe to put in front of a model or a person.

    Decoded leniently (a sandbox is under no obligation to emit valid UTF-8)
    and stripped of control characters, which are invisible on screen and
    are how text gets disguised on its way into a context window.
    """
    text = raw.decode("utf-8", errors="replace")
    return _CONTROL_RE.sub("", text)


class OutputRelay:
    """:func:`scrub_output`, fed one read at a time.

    It lives here rather than in a backend because the policy is the
    *seam's*: the cap, the lenient decode and the control strip are what
    make a command's output safe to put in front of a model or a person, and
    the next backend must reuse them rather than write a second version that
    agrees by coincidence. A backend supplies the reads; this decides what a
    consumer is told.

    **The decoder is incremental, and that is the whole reason this is an
    object.** A pipe splits wherever the kernel likes, so a multi-byte
    character routinely straddles two reads. Decoding each read on its own
    replaces both halves with U+FFFD, and the text somebody was streamed
    stops being the text the result carries — measured: the three characters
    of ``一二三`` cut after two bytes decode per-read as two replacement
    characters followed by ``二三``. The control strip needs no such care,
    because ``_CONTROL_RE`` matches one code point at a time and stripping
    per read is therefore the same strip; it happens here anyway, so that
    one object is the whole of "untrusted bytes become text somebody may be
    shown".

    **Nothing past ``limit`` is ever handed over.** A bounded read loop
    overshoots its cap by up to one read, so that "exactly at the cap" can
    be told from "over it", and the result is sliced back to the cap. Handing
    the overshoot on would put text on a person's screen that the model was
    never shown and the result does not contain.

    **The consumer gets a share of ``deadline`` and no more.** ``limit``
    bounds the BYTES a consumer is handed; it says nothing about the TIME it
    spends holding them, and those are different resources with different
    ways of running out. A consumer runs on the thread that called ``exec``,
    inside the read loop, so its time is time the command does not get and
    time the caller does not get back — and 64 KiB arrives in as many
    separate reads as the container feels like writing, so bytes do not
    bound calls either. Once it has spent :data:`SLOW_CONSUMER_SHARE` of the
    deadline it is not called again for the rest of that command: the same
    latch as a consumer that raised, for the same reason, at the same cost —
    the tail of somebody's transcript, and nothing of the command's.

    **What that does not bound is a call already in progress.** Nothing in
    this process can interrupt a function running on its own thread, so a
    consumer that never returns holds the thread, and no arrangement of
    checks here changes it. The budget bounds how much time this hands over,
    not how long a hand-over lasts. See ``exec``'s contract.

    **It never raises.** Feeding it is the last thing between a command and
    a pipe nobody is draining, so a consumer's exception must not travel
    back into the read loop. See :meth:`_say`.
    """

    def __init__(self, on_output, limit: int = MAX_OUTPUT_BYTES,
                 deadline: float = DEFAULT_EXEC_TIMEOUT):
        self._on_output = validate_on_output(on_output)
        self._room = limit
        # The command's deadline, not the consumer's share of it: the share
        # is this module's policy and is applied in one place, so that a
        # backend passes the number it already has and cannot pass a
        # different fraction. It defaults to the default deadline rather
        # than to "unbounded", because a relay nobody gave a deadline to is
        # a mistake that must still end.
        self._deadline = deadline
        self._budget = deadline * SLOW_CONSUMER_SHARE
        self._decoder = codecs.getincrementaldecoder("utf-8")("replace")
        #: Whether the consumer raised. It is not called again once it has.
        self.failed = False
        #: Whether the consumer spent its share of the deadline. It is not
        #: called again once it has, and a timeout is allowed to name it.
        self.overran = False
        #: Wall-clock seconds spent inside the consumer. That time comes out
        #: of the command's own deadline, so a timeout has to be able to say
        #: how much of it was not the command's fault.
        self.elapsed = 0.0

    def feed(self, block: bytes) -> None:
        """Hand on one read's worth of a running command's output."""
        if self._decoder is None or self._room <= 0:
            return
        if len(block) > self._room:
            block = block[:self._room]
        self._room -= len(block)
        self._say(self._decoder.decode(block))

    def close(self) -> None:
        """Say the last of it: a character something cut in half.

        Deliberately **not** gated on the remaining room. When the cap lands
        mid-character the result's own decode turns that tail into exactly
        one replacement character, so a relay that stopped at the last whole
        byte would have streamed one character less than the result carries
        — an off-by-one that no test written in ASCII can see.

        Called whatever happened, including when the command hit its
        deadline: what it printed before that is what somebody has already
        been shown, and the decoder's last partial character belongs with it.
        Safe to call twice.
        """
        if self._decoder is None:
            return
        tail = self._decoder.decode(b"", True)
        self._decoder = None
        self._say(tail)

    def _say(self, text):
        if self._on_output is None or self.failed or self.overran:
            return
        text = _CONTROL_RE.sub("", text)
        # A read that was half a character decodes to nothing at all, and a
        # consumer called with "" would spend one of its flush decisions on
        # no output.
        if not text:
            return
        started = time.monotonic()
        try:
            self._on_output(text)
        except Exception:
            # Once, and then never again for this command. A consumer that
            # raised on one read raises on every read, and a traceback per
            # read is its own outage — log spam is how a real incident gets
            # buried. What is lost is the tail of somebody's transcript; the
            # command is unaffected, its whole output still comes back on the
            # result, and this is where it says so.
            self.failed = True
            logger.exception(
                "the output consumer raised, so the rest of this command's "
                "output will not be handed to it. The command itself is "
                "unaffected and its whole output is still on the result.")
        finally:
            self.elapsed += time.monotonic() - started
            self._spend()

    def _spend(self):
        """Latch the consumer off once it has spent its share of the clock.

        Checked AFTER a call and not before the next one, because the call
        that overruns is usually the first: a consumer that blocked for a
        minute has to be stopped by what it has already spent, or a relay
        fed once — a command that printed one line and hung — leaves the
        latch unset and pays for that consumer a second time on `close`.

        It cannot stop a call already in progress; see the class docstring.
        This bounds the number of times a command's output is allowed to
        cost the caller's thread, which is the part that is boundable.

        There is deliberately no guard here against being called once the
        latch is set. :meth:`_say` returns before its ``try`` when it is, so
        this cannot be reached twice, and a second check that reads like the
        thing keeping the warning to one line — while being unreachable, and
        so unable to fail — is worse than no check: the next reader moves
        the real latch and trusts this one.
        """
        if self.elapsed < self._budget:
            return
        self.overran = True
        if self.failed:
            # It has already said why it stopped, and once is enough: two
            # records for one event is how the log stops being read.
            return
        logger.warning(
            "the output consumer held the thread for %.1fs, past its %.0f%% "
            "share of this command's %.1fs deadline, so the rest of this "
            "command's output will not be handed to it. It runs inside the "
            "read loop, so that time came out of the command's own deadline. "
            "The command is unaffected and its whole output is still on the "
            "result.",
            self.elapsed, SLOW_CONSUMER_SHARE * 100, self._deadline)


def bump(handle: Handle, ttl: float, now: float) -> Handle:
    """A handle with its deadline pushed out. See ``keepalive``."""
    return replace(handle, expires_at=now + validate_ttl(ttl))
